course: dictionary
lesson: 6044
title: Words that help us remember
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A memorial is something that helps people remember a person who has died, or something important that happened. It can be a statue, a stone, a building or an event. «The village built a war memorial with the names of soldiers carved on it.» The stone is there so nobody forgets them.
explain: Memorial can also be a describing word. A memorial service is held to remember someone after they have died. Read the words around it to work out which job the word is doing. Here the clues are «service» and «died», so you know it is about remembering someone.
explain: Some memorials are for everyone to visit. «Families sometimes pay for a bench, tree or plaque in a place the person loved.» Sitting on a memorial bench is fine, because that is what it is for. The context tells you how to behave there.
fact: A memorial is a statue, stone, building or event that helps people remember.
fact: Memorial can be a describing word, as in memorial garden: a garden made in someone's memory.
fact: We say «in memory of» someone to mean we are remembering them.
fact: «Memorial park» is an American word for a cemetery, a place where people are buried.
fact: On Remembrance Day, people lay poppies at war memorials to remember soldiers who died.
exercise: «The village built a war memorial with the names of soldiers carved on it.» What is a memorial here? | A tree planted for a sports day | A school bell | *A stone that helps people remember the soldiers | A shop selling flowers
exercise: In a «memorial service», people gather to remember someone who has died. What does memorial describe here? | Something that is for sale | *Something made in someone's memory | Something new and shiny | Something very large and tall
exercise: Which phrase means you are remembering someone? | *in memory of | in memorial of | with memorial for | at memorial to
test: «Being noisy at funerals and memorials can hurt grieving people for a long time.» How should you behave at a memorial? | Running about and shouting | *Quiet and respectful | Telling jokes to everyone | Loud and silly
test: What is a «memorial park» in American English? | A football pitch | A playground with swings | *A cemetery, a place where people are buried | A zoo for animals
exam: «On 11 November whole villages stand in silence at the war memorial.» Why do they stand in silence? | To sell food to visitors | To shout to their friends | To wait for the bus | *To remember the people who died
